# Soouls Multi-Platform Offline-First Architecture Plan

This document outlines the technical blueprint to package the **Soouls Next.js Web App** into high-performance, low-memory native applications for **Desktop** (Windows, macOS) and **Mobile** (iOS, Android) with **100% offline capability** and automatic background synchronization.

---

## 🏗️ Technical Architecture Overview

To achieve a **"Write Once, Run Everywhere"** workflow where a single change to the React frontend simultaneously updates all platforms, we will leverage **Tauri v2** for Desktop and **Capacitor** for Mobile.

```mermaid
graph TD
    subgraph Frontend Core [apps/frontend (Next.js App Router)]
        React[React / TypeScript Components]
        State[React Query & Offline Queue]
        IDB[(IndexedDB Cache)]
    end

    subgraph Native Wrappers
        Tauri[Tauri v2 Shell] -->|Rust Bridge| Desktop[Windows / macOS App]
        Capacitor[Capacitor Shell] -->|Web Native Bridge| Mobile[iOS / Android App]
    end

    React -.-> Tauri
    React -.-> Capacitor
    Desktop -->|Low RAM < 40MB| WinMac[Windows / macOS Native Webview]
    Mobile -->|Native Webview| iOSAndroid[iOS / Android WebView]
```

### Key Architectural Pillars:

1. **Lightweight & High Performance (Tauri v2)**:
   - Unlike Electron (which bundles a full Chromium instance and consumes 500MB+ RAM), **Tauri** is written in **Rust** and utilizes the operating system's native webview (WebView2 on Windows, WebKit on macOS).
   - This keeps memory usage extremely low (**typically 30–50MB of RAM**) and ensures lightning-fast startup times.
2. **Instant Cross-Platform Updates**:
   - Both Tauri and Capacitor load the static files built from your Next.js application.
   - During development, they connect to `http://localhost:3000` with **Hot Module Replacement (HMR)** enabled. If you change a component in `apps/frontend`, the change renders instantly in your browser, your Windows app, your macOS app, and mobile simulators!
3. **Offline-First Security & Autonomy**:
   - The UI is statically exported using Next.js Static Exports (`output: 'export'`). The HTML, CSS, and JS files are bundled directly *inside* the native app.
   - The app can load, run, and display your entries **completely offline without any internet connection**.

---

## 💾 Offline-First Sync Engine Design

To support seamless offline writing and automatic syncing, we will implement a lightweight **IndexedDB Sync Queue** using `idb-keyval` (already a dependency in the frontend!).

```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor User as User
    participant App as App Frontend (IndexedDB)
    participant Net as Connection Monitor
    participant API as tRPC Backend API
    participant AI as AI Engine (Clustering)

    User->>App: Writes new journal entry
    App->>App: Saves entry to IndexedDB ("local_entries")
    App->>App: Adds item to "sync_queue"
    App->>User: Shows "Stored locally (Offline)" badge
    
    Note over Net, API: Internet connection is restored
    Net->>App: Triggers "online" event
    App->>App: Processes "sync_queue" sequentially
    App->>API: Sends offline entries to backend
    API->>AI: Triggers background clustering & insights
    API-->>App: Confirms save & returns updated database entry
    App->>App: Removes item from "sync_queue"
    App->>User: Shows "Synced with cloud" + new AI Insights!
```

### Step 1: Client-Side Connection Monitoring

We will create a custom React hook `useOfflineStatus` to listen to the browser's connectivity events:

```typescript
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export function useOfflineStatus() {
  const [isOffline, setIsOffline] = useState(
    typeof window !== 'undefined' ? !navigator.onLine : false
  );

  useEffect(() => {
    if (typeof window === 'undefined') return;

    const handleOnline = () => setIsOffline(false);
    const handleOffline = () => setIsOffline(true);

    window.addEventListener('online', handleOnline);
    window.addEventListener('offline', handleOffline);

    return () => {
      window.removeEventListener('online', handleOnline);
      window.removeEventListener('offline', handleOffline);
    };
  }, []);

  return isOffline;
}
```

### Step 2: The IndexedDB Storage Engine

When the user is writing offline, we bypass direct backend tRPC calls and save entries to a local IndexedDB buffer. 

We will create a synchronization manager `src/utils/sync-manager.ts`:

```typescript
import { get, set, del, keys } from 'idb-keyval';

export interface PendingSyncEntry {
  id: string; // Unique client-side uuid
  title: string;
  content: string;
  createdAt: string;
  isDeleted?: boolean;
}

const SYNC_QUEUE_KEY = 'soouls_sync_queue';
const LOCAL_ENTRIES_KEY = 'soouls_local_entries';

// Save entry locally when offline
export async function saveEntryOffline(entry: PendingSyncEntry) {
  // 1. Update local cache for instant UI rendering
  const localEntries = (await get<PendingSyncEntry[]>(LOCAL_ENTRIES_KEY)) || [];
  const updatedLocal = [entry, ...localEntries.filter(e => e.id !== entry.id)];
  await set(LOCAL_ENTRIES_KEY, updatedLocal);

  // 2. Add to sync queue
  const queue = (await get<PendingSyncEntry[]>(SYNC_QUEUE_KEY)) || [];
  const updatedQueue = [...queue.filter(e => e.id !== entry.id), entry];
  await set(SYNC_QUEUE_KEY, updatedQueue);
}

// Synchronize queue with NestJS backend when online
export async function syncOfflineQueue(createEntryMutation: any) {
  const queue = await get<PendingSyncEntry[]>(SYNC_QUEUE_KEY);
  if (!queue || queue.length === 0) return;

  for (const entry of queue) {
    try {
      // Send to server
      await createEntryMutation.mutateAsync({
        title: entry.title,
        content: entry.content,
        createdAt: entry.createdAt,
      });

      // Remove from sync queue upon success
      const currentQueue = (await get<PendingSyncEntry[]>(SYNC_QUEUE_KEY)) || [];
      const updatedQueue = currentQueue.filter(e => e.id !== entry.id);
      await set(SYNC_QUEUE_KEY, updatedQueue);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Failed to sync entry, retrying later:', error);
      break; // Stop queue processing if server fails (e.g. timeout)
    }
  }
}
```

